   "nomor": 2,
   "judul": "Volunteering at Kingsley City Farm",
   "instruksi": "Questions 11-20. Listen to the talk for new volunteers and answer the questions below.\nQuestions 15-20. Complete the notes below. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.",
   "bacaan": null,
   "transkrip": "VOICES: narrator=female\nGood evening, everyone, and thank you for giving up your Thursday to come and hear about volunteering at Kingsley City Farm.\nLet me start with what the farm actually is, because people often arrive expecting a petting zoo. Kingsley was set up in 1978 on land that had been a coal yard, and the original purpose was not to entertain children but to give people in the surrounding streets somewhere to grow food. The animals came later, in the mid-eighties, and although they are now what most visitors photograph, the growing beds are still the heart of the place.\nWe currently have about ninety volunteers on the books, and roughly thirty of those come in every week. What we need most at the moment is not more people at weekends — Saturdays are, frankly, crowded — but people who can give us a weekday morning. Tuesday and Wednesday mornings are when the deliveries arrive and when almost all the maintenance gets done.\nNow, the roles. Animal care is the one everybody asks about, and I should be honest with you: it is the hardest to get into, because volunteers who start there tend to stay for years. Gardening is where we always have space, and it is also, I would argue, where you will learn the most. Then there's the shop, the café, and school visits. School visits need people who are comfortable talking to a group of thirty seven-year-olds, which is a particular skill.\nEverybody, whatever their role, does the same half-day induction. You cannot start without it. It covers handwashing, what to do if a child is hurt, and where the fire assembly point is. We run it on the first Saturday of each month.\nA few practical things. Wear boots you don't mind ruining. We provide gloves, but not footwear. Do not bring food onto the site for the animals — well-meaning visitors have made several of our goats seriously ill this way. And if you are going to be absent, please tell us the day before, because on a weekday morning we may only have four people on site and losing one of them changes what we can do.\nFinally, the thing nobody expects me to say: the reason most volunteers leave is not the weather and not the mud. It is that they took on too much at the start. Begin with one session a fortnight. You can always add more.",
