   "judul": "A tutorial about a field study",
   "instruksi": "Questions 21-30. Listen to the discussion between two students and their tutor, and answer the questions below.\nQuestions 26-30. Complete the sentences below. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.",
   "bacaan": null,
   "transkrip": "VOICES: TUTOR=male, PRIYA=female, MARCUS=male\nTUTOR: Right, Priya, Marcus — how is the river study coming along?\nPRIYA: Better than last week. We've settled on the Elder Brook rather than the Calder, because the Calder has three sewage outflows on it and we'd never be able to say what was causing what.\nTUTOR: That's sound reasoning. Fewer variables. And what exactly are you measuring?\nMARCUS: Three things at each site: water temperature, dissolved oxygen, and the number of invertebrate species.\nTUTOR: And the third of those is the interesting one, isn't it? Why?\nMARCUS: Because it's the only one that tells you about the last few months rather than the last few hours. Temperature and oxygen change between morning and afternoon. The invertebrates are a record.\nTUTOR: Exactly. Now, how many sites?\nPRIYA: We had planned twelve, but we've cut it to eight. We realised that twelve sites done badly is worth less than eight done properly.\nTUTOR: I agree, and I would have said so if you hadn't. Eight is enough for the statistics you'll want to run. What's your sampling method?\nPRIYA: Kick sampling, three minutes at each site, same person doing it every time.\nTUTOR: Good — the same person is important, and students always forget it. Now, the part I want to press you on. What is your control?\nMARCUS: We're using the stretch above the farm as the control, because nothing enters the water upstream of it.\nTUTOR: Are you sure? Have either of you actually walked that stretch?\nPRIYA: Not all of it, no.\nTUTOR: Then that's your next job, before you collect a single sample. I have seen three projects fail in the same way: a control site that turned out to have a drain in it. Walk the whole stretch, photograph anything that enters the water, and only then decide.\nMARCUS: And the write-up — how long should the method section be?\nTUTOR: Long enough that somebody else could repeat exactly what you did, and no longer. Most people write too much there and too little in the discussion. Aim for a thousand words on method, and remember the deadline is the fourteenth of May — that's a Friday, and the office closes at four.",
