   "judul": "Urban heat islands",
   "instruksi": "Questions 31-40. Complete the lecture notes below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.",
   "bacaan": null,
   "transkrip": "VOICES: narrator=male\nGood afternoon. Today I want to talk about a phenomenon that most of you have felt without naming: the urban heat island.\nThe term describes the simple observation that a city is warmer than the countryside around it. The difference is not trivial. On a still, clear night, the centre of a large city can be seven degrees warmer than farmland twelve kilometres away. And note that I said night. During the day the difference is often small; it is after sunset that the gap opens, because the city is not so much heating up faster as cooling down more slowly.\nWhy? Three reasons, and I want you to be able to separate them.\nThe first is materials. Concrete, brick and asphalt are dense, and dense materials store heat. A field radiates its warmth away within an hour or two of sunset. A street of buildings releases it all night.\nThe second is geometry. A narrow street between tall buildings is what climatologists call an urban canyon, and it behaves like one: heat radiating from a wall does not escape to the sky, it simply strikes the wall opposite. The narrower the street, the less sky each surface can see, and the more slowly it cools.\nThe third is that cities produce heat of their own — from engines, from air conditioning, from bodies. In dense districts this can be a surprisingly large share of the total.\nAnd there is a fourth factor that works by absence rather than presence: vegetation. A tree does not merely provide shade. It also cools the air by transpiration, moving water from soil to leaf to atmosphere, and that transfer takes energy. Replace grass with tarmac and you remove that cooling entirely.\nNow, why does this matter beyond comfort? Heat is the deadliest weather hazard in most temperate countries, more so than storms or floods, and the deaths are concentrated at night. A body recovers from daytime heat while asleep — unless the bedroom does not cool. That is the mechanism by which the heat island kills.\nWhat can be done? Three approaches, in ascending order of usefulness. Painting roofs a lighter colour raises reflectivity, and it works, though it does nothing at street level. Green roofs are better, but they are heavy and most existing buildings cannot take one. The measure with the strongest evidence behind it is also the oldest: planting street trees. A mature canopy over a residential street reduces the surface temperature of the road beneath it by up to twelve degrees, and it does so at a cost that any city can afford.",
   "audioBerkas": "cb1af6bd29812dc2.wav",
   "audioNama": "recording-4.wav"
